<div class="container py-5">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
        <div class="col-lg-10">
            <!-- Header Banner -->
            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 bg-dark-gradient text-white p-4 p-md-5 mb-5">
                <span class="badge bg-success-subtle text-success border border-success-subtle px-3 py-1 rounded-pill align-self-start mb-3">
                    Project Documentation & Technical Specifications
                </span>
                <h1 class="display-5 fw-extrabold mb-3">BreedVision: Indigenous Bovine Recognition</h1>
                <p class="lead text-light-emphasis mb-0">
                    An end-to-end full-stack AI web application built with ASP.NET Core MVC, C#, ONNX Runtime, and SQL Server for real-time identification and phenotypic characterization of Indian cattle and river buffalo breeds.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Project Background & Need -->
            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 p-4 p-md-5 bg-white mb-4">
                <h3 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-3"><i class="bi bi-geo-alt-fill text-danger me-2"></i> Project Significance & Problem Statement</h3>
                <p class="text-secondary">
                    India possesses one of the world's richest reserves of indigenous bovine genetic biodiversity. The Indian Council of Agricultural Research – National Bureau of Animal Genetic Resources (ICAR-NBAGR) registers and tracks indigenous breeds of cattle (<em>Bos indicus</em>) and riverine buffaloes (<em>Bubalus bubalis</em>).
                </p>
                <p class="text-secondary">
                    Accurate identification of indigenous breeds directly impacts breed conservation, selective artificial insemination, livestock insurance verification, and dairy productivity management. However, field officers and rural dairy farmers often lack immediate morphological taxonomy expertise to differentiate closely related zebu or buffalo breeds. <strong>BreedVision</strong> addresses this gap by offering instant, locally executable, and verifiable AI-assisted breed recognition from mobile and digital photographs.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Deep Learning Architecture & Mathematical Pipeline -->
            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 p-4 p-md-5 bg-white mb-4">
                <h3 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-4"><i class="bi bi-cpu-fill text-success me-2"></i> Machine Learning Pipeline & Mathematics</h3>
                
                <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-light">
                            <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-2"><i class="bi bi-layers-half text-primary me-1"></i> ResNet-50 Feature Extractor</h6>
                            <p class="small text-secondary mb-0">
                                The core neural network is a 50-layer deep convolutional neural network (CNN) exported to <strong>Open Neural Network Exchange (ONNX)</strong> format. It receives a standardized RGB tensor $[1, 3, 224, 224]$ and extracts an uncompressed 2048-dimensional high-level feature vector $\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{2048}$.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-light">
                            <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-2"><i class="bi bi-bullseye text-success me-1"></i> Metric Learning & Breed Prototypes</h6>
                            <p class="small text-secondary mb-0">
                                Rather than relying on rigid static classification layers, the model employs metric learning. Pre-computed 2048-dimensional centroid prototype vectors $\mathbf{p}_k$ for each of the 10 breeds are evaluated using cosine similarity:
                                $$\text{Sim}(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{p}_k) = \frac{\mathbf{z} \cdot \mathbf{p}_k}{\|\mathbf{z}\|_2 \|\mathbf{p}_k\|_2}$$
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-light">
                            <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-2"><i class="bi bi-calculator-fill text-warning me-1"></i> Calibrated Softmax Confidence</h6>
                            <p class="small text-secondary mb-0">
                                True probabilistic scores are computed via temperature-scaled softmax over all breed similarities:
                                $$P(\text{Breed}_k) = \frac{e^{\text{Sim}(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{p}_k) / T}}{\sum_{j=1}^{10} e^{\text{Sim}(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{p}_j) / T}} \times 100\%$$
                                where temperature $T = 0.08$ produces calibrated probabilities matching empirical multi-class distributions.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="border rounded-3 p-3 h-100 bg-light">
                            <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-2"><i class="bi bi-shield-x text-danger me-1"></i> Out-Of-Distribution (OOD) Protection</h6>
                            <p class="small text-secondary mb-0">
                                To prevent false certainty on non-livestock, non-bovine, or invalid images, an empirical rejection boundary is enforced: if $\max_k \text{Sim} < 0.68$ or Top-1 confidence $< 40\%$, the system automatically flags the prediction as <em>Uncertain / Low Confidence Detection</em>.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

